months leading up to 9 11 the cia  sounded the alarm that something was  coming a new cbs news documentary that  airs tonight takes us inside the cia's  hunt for al qaeda and its then leader  osama bin laden this is a preview  [Music]  from  april to  mid-july of 2001  there was a significant increase  in intelligence reporting about al-qaeda  planning attacks  catastrophic attacks  multiple simultaneous attacks  history changing attacks  we'd seen chatter before other  operations  but they started using language we had  never heard before they were calling it  olympic sized  armageddon  there were real plots being manifested  the world felt like it was on the edge  of eruption  it's early this tuesday morning the 11th  of september 2001  [Music]  from the heart of new york city the  first job of any government is to  protect the country and protect its  people  but you don't get credit for what you  stopped  if you don't stop them all  [Music]  and we were unable to stop the big one  [Music]  for us  there was a personal dimension  3 000 americans have been killed  the city i grew up in had been blown up  there was an emotional piece of this  because we had woken up every day of our  lives  trying to stop what just had happened  it's still so emotional we're joined now  by former acting and deputy cia director  michael morell whom you saw in that clip  20 years ago he served as the cia's  presidential daily briefer and was with  president george w bush on 9 11. he is  now cbs news senior national security  contributor and joins us at the table  mike it's good to see you great to see  you you know i'd forgotten that you were  with the president that day and you were  there at that school because we didn't  even know what we were looking at but  here's a question you're the perfect  person to be here today because your  knowledge is so vast the question is do  you do you think we're safer today than  we were back then  we are absolutely safer today than we  were back then gail  for two reasons one is  immediately after 9 11 we increased our  defenses so it is much more difficult  for terrorists to get into the united  states it's much more difficult for them  to move money is much more difficult for  anybody to hijack an aircraft  the second thing we did is we took the  fight to them overseas  so wherever we could find them whether  they were in afghanistan whether they  were in pakistan whether they were in  yemen we took the fight to them and  degraded them so that they didn't have  the capability anymore to attack us so  and we've done that consistently since 9  11. but now the taliban's in control in  afghanistan that's why i asked the  question do you think we're safer  because for a lot of people it seems  like we're right back where we started  right do you think that it's possible  that al qaeda could re-emerge  yes and whether that happens depends  entirely on us  so i told president bush on 9 11 that i  was  highly confident that what just happened  was the result of bin laden  and al qaeda i'm here to tell you today  that i am highly confident that the  taliban will allow al-qaeda to have safe  haven in afghanistan and al-qaeda will  want to reconstitute so the question is  will we allow that will we have the  intelligence capabilities to watch them  to see what they're doing  look at their plans and intentions look  at their capabilities and then will we  have the military capability to go in  there and degrade them  if we need to and will we have the will  to do that  if we do that  they won't be a threat if we don't they  could be a threat within 12 months do  you consider al qaeda and the situation  in afghanistan to be the single biggest  threat facing america today so not right  now right i worry about al-shabab and  somalia i worry about isis in north  africa i worry about domestic terrorism  here  but if we don't do what we need to do in  afghanistan it will become the single  biggest threat again what will be the  legacy of our war in afghanistan  so i think about it as what's the legacy  of the entire war on terror right and i  think there's two pieces to it one is we  have protected the homeland since 9 11.  there hasn't been another 911. we all we  all worked very hard to make sure that  that didn't happen that's fantastic  that's success right we won that war  but at the same time  i think we've weakened america a little  bit because for those 20 years that we  were fighting a counter-terrorism war  and a counter-insurgency war the  russians and the chinese were catching  up to us conventionally and some of the  things we did post-911 the war in iraq  for example  caused political  division here at home right that we're  still paying for right all right michael  morell you have the last word on that  thank you very much for joining us today  race against time the cia and 911 a  production of our very own see it now  studios airs tonight at 8 7 central  right here on cbs and it's available  tomorrow on the viacom cbs streaming  service paramount plus
 

